{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "Nothing is at stake but your own time, and the artifact is meant to be deleted, so unread code costs you only a cheaply explored idea. Take level 4 freely, but write down that it will be thrown away -- if you catch yourself adding a database or auth flow you plan to keep, it has stopped being a prototype and the level drops."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo is a claim about what the product can do, so it has to actually do that on the day, in front of people whose decisions you care about; that needs behavior checks, not line-by-line polish. Level 3 buys reliability where it shows while keeping the artifact disposable, but a working demo is not evidence that the underlying architecture is sound."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Real users bring real data and the first real cost of being wrong, but you still need speed and should expect to rewrite; selective review plus tests that exercise the actual user journeys is the right trade. Escalate auth, payments, and anything touching personal data to level 2 regardless of what the rest of the MVP is at."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Paying customers and an on-call team mean every defect has a price and a maintainer, so the default is a person reading every change -- the same bar you would set for a human colleague. Level 3 is defensible only in isolated, well-tested, low-blast-radius areas, and even there the named reviewer owns the change."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "Regulated and safety-critical code carries an obligation to show who wrote what and why, and frameworks such as IEC 62304 or DO-178C assume human authorship and review evidence that \"the model wrote it and the tests passed\" does not satisfy. Treat 1 as the ceiling -- AI as a drafting assistant whose output a qualified engineer rewrites and signs -- and drop to 0 for safety kernels and anything whose failure injures someone."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Set the level from blast radius and lifetime, not from enthusiasm: the cheaper a mistake is to erase and the fewer people it can reach, the more you can let the model run. The rule that matters most is that code never gets promoted at its current level -- anything graduating to real users gets re-read at the new stage's bar, because the classic failure is a level-4 prototype quietly becoming production. Treat each number as a ceiling for that area rather than a target to hit."
}

